Summary
Key Concepts
1. Image References
•	Tags: Human-readable labels assigned by image developers (e.g., v1.0, latest).
•	SHA IDs: Immutable identifiers automatically computed by the registry. Always uniquely identifies an image.
2. Deployment Image Management
•	imagePullPolicy: Specifies how pods pull images from the registry:
o	Always – pull every time the pod starts.
o	IfNotPresent – pull only if not already on node.
o	Never – never pull, use local image.
•	Deployment strategies determine how updates affect running applications:
o	RollingUpdate: Gradually replaces old pods with new ones.
o	Recreate: Terminates all old pods before starting new ones.
3. OpenShift Image Streams
•	Provide stable references to images.
•	Image streams exist within a project.
•	Local lookup policy must be enabled to let Kubernetes workloads resolve images using image stream tags.
4. Workload Integration
•	Kubernetes resources like Deployments, Jobs, and CronJobs can use image streams.
•	Image streams decouple workloads from registry-specific image names, improving reproducibility.
5. Automatic Updates
•	Image triggers can be configured in deployments:
o	Automatically roll out new pods when an image stream tag is updated.
o	Works with versioned tags and aliases.
o	Allows safe rollback by reverting the image stream tag.
6. Benefits
•	Ensures reproducible deployments.
•	Enables controlled rollouts and automatic updates.
•	Provides flexibility in pointing multiple workloads to the same image via tags or aliases.

